# infra/prod/Caddyfile.example — DIRECT (non-Cloudflare) access to the panel.
#
# Serves the panel on a DNS-only host — an A/AAAA record pointing STRAIGHT to this
# server, NOT proxied by Cloudflare — with automatic Let's Encrypt TLS. Users who
# cannot reach Cloudflare open this host and sign in via the panel's own Google
# login (server-side). Cloudflare Access on the main host keeps working too.
#
# This file is the "port 443 is free" method (INSTALL.md §3.5 method A). If :443 is
# already owned by another service (a site, a VPN edge, an haproxy/nginx SNI
# router), do NOT use auto-HTTPS here: route the host through that terminator to a
# local Caddy on a loopback port and give Caddy a STATIC cert instead, e.g.:
#     { https_port 7443 }
#     direct.example.com {
#         bind 127.0.0.1
#         reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:5430
#         tls /etc/caddy/certs/direct.fullchain.pem /etc/caddy/certs/direct.key.pem
#     }
# (obtain the cert out-of-band, e.g. acme.sh HTTP-01, with a renew+reload hook).
# See INSTALL.md §3.5 method B.
#
# Setup:
#   1. Add a DNS-only A record, e.g. direct.example.com -> this server's IP
#      (grey cloud in Cloudflare, or any registrar). Port 80/443 must be open.
#   2. Install Caddy, put this file at /etc/caddy/Caddyfile (edit the host below
#      and the email), then: systemctl reload caddy
#   3. In infra/prod/.env set PANEL_PUBLIC_URL=https://direct.example.com plus the
#      GOOGLE_OAUTH_* and PANEL_IDENTITY_SECRET values, and recreate web +
#      control-api. Add https://direct.example.com/api/auth/google/callback as an
#      Authorized redirect URI on the Google OAuth client.

{
	# Let's Encrypt registration email (for expiry notices).
	email admin@example.com
}

direct.example.com {
	encode zstd gzip
	# The panel's web port (loopback, same as the Cloudflare Tunnel targets).
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:5430
}
